/*
 * EXEMPLAR — Form B: transactional outbox + relay.
 * The counterpart of `KafkaProducerAdapter.java.example` (Form A, publish after commit).
 * Which form applies is decided in `SKILL.md` step 3, first axis, against the criterion in
 * `.claude/rules/messaging.md` § Publication timing. Form A is the default; this file is
 * for the case where losing the event between the commit and the publish is not acceptable.
 * Form reference for the executor agent; not a file to copy verbatim.
 * Rules: `.claude/rules/messaging.md` (§ Publication timing, § Delivery semantics)
 *        `.claude/rules/architecture-ddd.md` (Adapters, Application)
 *        `.claude/rules/persistence.md` (the table itself)
 *        `.claude/rules/observability.md` (§ Metrics — the dead-letter counter and the
 *        pending-age gauge below are required, not decoration)
 *
 * WHAT THIS EXEMPLAR SHOWS:
 *  - the outbound port's implementation writes a row instead of sending: it runs inside the
 *    caller's transaction, so state and intent-to-publish commit or roll back together;
 *  - the relay is a separate component and the only place that talks to the broker;
 *  - the relay reaches outbox state through `OutboxRelayGateway`, an application-layer
 *    abstraction — never through the persistence adapter's JPA entity or Spring Data
 *    repository, which live in a sibling adapter it may not import;
 *  - duplicates are expected by design (send succeeds, mark fails) and are absorbed by the
 *    idempotent consumer the rule already requires;
 *  - the whole component switches off with one property, which is what keeps it out of the
 *    integration tests — see SCHEDULING below.
 *
 * SCHEDULING, AND WHY IT IS CONDITIONAL. `@Scheduled` does nothing without
 * `@EnableScheduling` somewhere in the context, so Form B ships `SchedulingConfig` below:
 * without it the relay is inert and the whole form silently does not deliver. Both the config
 * and the relay are gated on `app.outbox.enabled`, defaulted on. That property is not
 * ceremony — it is what the test profile sets to `false`. Every `*IT` is a
 * `@SpringBootTest` and boots this configuration too, so an ungated relay polls the test
 * database every second, blocks on `KafkaTemplate.send(...).join()` against a broker no test
 * starts (Testcontainers provides the database, not a broker), and marks rows the test just
 * created as failed or dead-lettered under it. That happened: an integration test asserting
 * `claimPending` contains the row it produced passed on timing luck, and the relay's only
 * line coverage came from the scheduler running it where nothing asserted anything —
 * lessons-learned-013 § 3. `src/test/resources/application-test.yml` carries
 * `app.outbox.enabled: false`, and a test that wants the relay turns it on and provides a
 * mocked `KafkaTemplate`.
 *
 * SCHEMA REQUIREMENT, NOT DDL. `outbox_events` is shared by the whole project — one table,
 * not one per event, same nature as `idempotency_keys`. `persistence-architect` models it,
 * from the requirements the messaging partial's § 2 states:
 *   id · aggregate id (partition key source) · event type · payload · occurred at
 *   published at (null = pending)  · attempts  · last error  · dead lettered
 *   an index that serves the claim query alone — pending rows only, not the whole table
 * Writing the DDL here would put schema in two owners' hands; see `.claude/rules/persistence.md`.
 * That skill's step 4b models it from `persistence-architect/templates/OutboxEventTable.sql.example`
 * and `OutboxEventStore.java.example` — the second implements `OutboxRelayGateway` below, and
 * this file stays the port's only declaration.
 *
 * ONE CARRIER, BOTH DIRECTIONS. `OutboxEventRecord` below is what the appender writes and
 * what the relay reads. A run that invents a second, identical record for the write side
 * (`NewOutboxEvent` next to `PendingOutboxEvent`, same five components in the same order) has
 * written the same type twice — lessons-learned-013 § 8.3. When one direction genuinely gains
 * a component the other cannot carry, that is the moment the type splits, and the partial says
 * so; until then there is one.
 *
 * WHAT'S NOT HERE: the pruning job for published rows (a scheduled delete, sized by
 * retention, no messaging decision in it), and retry/DLQ of the broker send itself — that
 * stays `application-kafka.yml`'s `DefaultErrorHandler`, same as Form A.
 *
 * ELIDED, adapter-local and uninteresting: `OutboxAppender` (serializes the payload record
 * and inserts the row through the same outbound abstraction, in `…messaging.outbox` —
 * every flow appends through it, so it is shared infrastructure and `public`),
 * `TopicResolver` (event type to topic name, in `…messaging.outbox` for the same reason, per
 * `.claude/rules/messaging.md` § Topics and serialization), and `OrderConfirmedPayload` —
 * unchanged from Form A, in this flow's own `…messaging.orderconfirmed`, see
 * `KafkaProducerAdapter.java.example`. The payload shape does not depend on the publication
 * form. Those two are the exception the § Boundary rule allows by name: package-private is the
 * default, and a class another subpackage genuinely consumes opens up one at a time.
 *
 * PACKAGE: the outbox is shared transport infrastructure, not one event's flow, and that is
 * exactly why it gets its own subpackage instead of being dropped into the adapter root —
 * `…messaging.outbox` (`.claude/rules/messaging.md` § Boundary). A root that holds "whatever
 * belongs to nobody" is the flat package the rule exists to prevent. Its persistence half
 * lives under `…persistence.outbox` for the same reason, named as a pair on purpose: one
 * concern, two adapters, one subpackage name in each
 * (`persistence-architect/templates/OutboxEventStore.java.example`). The event's own payload
 * and its writer stay in the flow's subpackage; only what every event's relay shares lives
 * here. Package-private by default, as everywhere else in this adapter.
 *
 * Each `// --- <dir>/<File>.java` block is its own file, at that subpackage.
 */

// --- OutboxRelayGateway.java (application layer — the relay's only view of outbox state)
package com.example.demo.application.port.out;

import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.UUID;

public interface OutboxRelayGateway {

    /** Pending rows, oldest first, capped. Ordered per aggregate so partition order holds. */
    List<OutboxEventRecord> claimPending(int batchSize);

    void markPublished(UUID eventId);

    /**
     * Increments attempts; flags the row dead-lettered once maxAttempts is reached. Returns
     * whether THIS call is the one that dead-lettered it — the relay cannot see the row's
     * attempts count, and a counter that misses the main loss path measures nothing.
     */
    boolean markFailed(UUID eventId, String reason, int maxAttempts);

    /**
     * Flags the row dead-lettered now, without spending attempts: a failure that retrying
     * cannot fix, such as an event type no topic maps. Says what it means — passing
     * `maxAttempts = 0` to `markFailed` reaches the same state by arithmetic and reads as a
     * sentinel nobody can explain six months later (lessons-learned-013 § 8.4).
     */
    void markDeadLettered(UUID eventId, String reason);

    /**
     * When the oldest still-pending row occurred, or empty when nothing is pending. Feeds the
     * gauge below: a counter says loss already happened, this says delivery is falling behind
     * before it does.
     */
    Optional<Instant> oldestPendingOccurredAt();
}

// --- OutboxEventRecord.java (application layer — carries no JPA and no Kafka type)
package com.example.demo.application.port.out;

import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.UUID;

public record OutboxEventRecord(
        UUID eventId, String aggregateId, String eventType, String payload, Instant occurredAt) {}

// --- orderconfirmed/OrderConfirmedOutboxWriter.java (implements the port domain-modeling declared)
package com.example.demo.adapter.out.messaging.orderconfirmed;

import com.example.demo.application.port.out.PublishOrderConfirmedPort;
import com.example.demo.domain.event.OrderConfirmed;

/*
 * No `@Transactional` here: the use case owns the transaction
 * (`.claude/rules/architecture-ddd.md` § Application). This class only has to run inside it —
 * writing the row in its own transaction would recreate exactly the gap Form B closes.
 */
public class OrderConfirmedOutboxWriter implements PublishOrderConfirmedPort {

    private final OutboxAppender outbox;

    public OrderConfirmedOutboxWriter(OutboxAppender outbox) {
        this.outbox = outbox;
    }

    @Override
    public void publish(OrderConfirmed event) {
        outbox.append(
                event.orderId().value().toString(),
                "OrderConfirmed",
                OrderConfirmedPayload.from(event));
    }
}

// --- outbox/OutboxProperties.java (adapter-local binding of `app.outbox.*`)
package com.example.demo.adapter.out.messaging.outbox;

import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;

/*
 * `app.outbox.*` bound once, instead of an `@Value` per constant. WHICH VALUES exist and what
 * they should be is `persistence-architect`'s call — it owns the table and the pacing the
 * columns encode (`.claude/decisions/0057-lessons-learned-012-inferences.md`, item 9); the
 * binding itself is adapter-local, which is why the record lives here next to its only reader
 * and no messaging class imports a persistence type.
 *
 * `pollInterval` is NOT in this record and cannot be: `@Scheduled(fixedDelayString = ...)` is
 * read by the annotation processor before any bean exists, so it resolves the property
 * placeholder directly. A value bound here would be read by nothing while the schedule quietly
 * fell back to its default (lessons-learned-013 § 2). Keep the placeholder and this record in
 * agreement — the property is named once in each, and nowhere else.
 */
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.outbox")
public record OutboxProperties(boolean enabled, int batchSize, int maxAttempts) {

    public OutboxProperties {
        batchSize = batchSize > 0 ? batchSize : 100;
        maxAttempts = maxAttempts > 0 ? maxAttempts : 5;
    }
}

// --- outbox/SchedulingConfig.java (adapter-local framework wiring — the relay is the only @Scheduled)
package com.example.demo.adapter.out.messaging.outbox;

import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnProperty;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.EnableScheduling;

/*
 * `@EnableScheduling` lives next to the only component that needs it, not in a project-wide
 * config package: the same reason `KafkaProducerConfig` sits in the messaging adapter — a
 * Kafka-typed or relay-typed `@Configuration` is adapter-local wiring, and
 * `.claude/rules/messaging.md` § How to verify requires every Kafka-typed import to sit under
 * the messaging package.
 */
@Configuration
@EnableScheduling
@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "app.outbox.enabled", matchIfMissing = true)
class SchedulingConfig {}

// --- outbox/OutboxRelay.java (the only class in Form B that imports KafkaTemplate)
package com.example.demo.adapter.out.messaging.outbox;

import com.example.demo.application.port.out.OutboxEventRecord;
import com.example.demo.application.port.out.OutboxRelayGateway;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Counter;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Gauge;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import java.time.Clock;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletionException;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnProperty;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "app.outbox.enabled", matchIfMissing = true)
public class OutboxRelay {

    private final OutboxProperties properties;
    private final OutboxRelayGateway gateway;
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;
    private final TopicResolver topics;
    private final Clock clock;
    private final Counter deadLettered;

    public OutboxRelay(
            OutboxProperties properties,
            OutboxRelayGateway gateway,
            KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate,
            TopicResolver topics,
            Clock clock,
            MeterRegistry meters) {
        this.properties = properties;
        this.gateway = gateway;
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
        this.topics = topics;
        this.clock = clock;
        // An event the partial declares "must not be lost" and a failure path that only logs
        // is loss made silent and permanent — the exact property Form B was chosen to avoid
        // (lessons-learned-013 § 6). The counter says it happened; the gauge says it is about
        // to, which is the half a log line can never give.
        this.deadLettered = Counter.builder("outbox.events.dead_lettered")
                .description("Outbox rows abandoned after exhausting their attempts")
                .register(meters);
        Gauge.builder("outbox.pending.age.seconds", this::oldestPendingAgeSeconds)
                .description("Age of the oldest still-unpublished outbox row")
                .register(meters);
    }

    /*
     * Fixed delay, not fixed rate: a slow pass must not overlap the next one. Interval and
     * batch size come from configuration — `application-kafka.yml.example`, `app.outbox.*`.
     */
    @Scheduled(fixedDelayString = "${app.outbox.poll-interval:PT1S}")
    public void relay() {
        for (OutboxEventRecord event : gateway.claimPending(properties.batchSize())) {
            String topic = topics.forEventType(event.eventType());
            if (topic == null) {
                // No retry will map a type nobody registered. Dead-letter it by name, not by
                // an attempts count that happens to arrive at the same state.
                gateway.markDeadLettered(event.eventId(), "no topic mapped for " + event.eventType());
                deadLettered.increment();
                continue;
            }
            try {
                kafkaTemplate.send(topic, event.aggregateId(), event.payload()).join();
                gateway.markPublished(event.eventId());
            } catch (CompletionException e) {
                // `join()` wraps every send failure in this one type, which is also why the
                // catch is not `RuntimeException`: Checkstyle's IllegalCatch rejects that, and
                // the narrower type is what the call actually throws.
                // The send may well have succeeded; the consumer's dedupe is what makes the
                // retry safe. Never rethrow: one poisoned row must not stall the batch.
                if (gateway.markFailed(event.eventId(), e.getMessage(), properties.maxAttempts())) {
                    deadLettered.increment();
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private double oldestPendingAgeSeconds() {
        return gateway.oldestPendingOccurredAt()
                .map(occurredAt -> (double) Duration.between(occurredAt, clock.instant()).toSeconds())
                .orElse(0d);
    }
}
